  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nCool temperatures, light winds, and dew point depressions near \nzero may result in patchy fog early this morning, especially along\nthe Musselshell and lower Yellowstone River valleys (20 percent \nchance). Any fog that does develop should lift after sunrise, \ngiving way to a mild and dry day with high temperatures in the 50s\nto lower 60s. While winds will be lighter today than yesterday, \nexpect winds to shift and strengthen out of the northeast and east\nthrough the day. With this, peak wind gusts around 10 to 20 mph \nare forecast, strongest around Livingston. As the winds subside \ntonight, cool temperatures and low dew point depressions may \nresult in another round of patchy fog across south-central and \neastern Montana (20 percent chance). Any fog that develops is \nexpected to lift around to a little after sunrise on Friday.\n\nFriday into Saturday will remain mild and mainly dry, though the \nchance of precipitation does begin to increase over/near the \nmountains as higher atmospheric moisture content and a few waves \nof energy move through the region from the south. This is \nassociated with the low pressure system that is expected to be \ndeveloping off the west coast of the United States. While winds \nwill be breezy out of the southeast in eastern Montana on Friday, \ngenerally light winds are expected on Saturday. \n\nSunday into Monday, the previously mentioned low pressure system \nis expected to move onshore and through our region in some \nfashion, though uncertainty remains in the evolution of this \nsystem over our region. With that said, there is at least a chance\nof seeing brief cooler temperatures, precipitation, and windy \nconditions over our area as this system moves through. At this \ntime, the chance of seeing a half inch (0.50\") or more of \nprecipitation (rain and melted snow) is medium over the foothills \nof southern Montana and northern Wyoming and the plains of far \nsouth- eastern Montana, and high over the mountains of southern \nMontana and northern Wyoming. As far as precipitation type goes, \nensembles have been consistent showing this system being too warm,\nin most solutions, for snow over the plains. With that said, \ndynamic cooling and/or precipitation occurring at night could \nstill allow for snow over some of the higher hills in south- \neastern Montana and northern Wyoming (low chance). Otherwise, \ntemperatures will still be cool enough for snow in the mountains. \nFor wind, the National Blend of Models (NBM) is currently \nadvertising wind gusts in the 20s to 40s mph, with a low chance of\n50 mph or greater wind gusts. \n\nMainly dry conditions look to return Tuesday into Wednesday next \nweek, though a cold front may bring a brief shot of light \nprecipitation through the area late Tuesday into early Wednesday \n(overall low chance). LA",
